**Definition 8.5 (total finite failure routing and raw states).** Inherited
history/source parse, range, dependency and invocation failures retain
ARC's exact typed routes. A new handler failure occurs with idle mailbox
and handler invocation: retain literal binding/range failure 1000 and the
actual request; use ARC's full Rejection publication and its original
ClientImage rejection/ACK/failed-cycle/Ready route. A callback failure
already holding a receiver does not publish: retain 1000, append the
tagged actual fault on 71, ACK that channel once, return mailbox availability,
append failed cycle, return invocation and Ready. First failing test in
the written order determines the failure. No decoder fallback or retry
exists. Authentic publications cannot reach these failures by §9.

Withheld input is not a failure response. Busy second entry, missing token,
unsealed read or disabled boundary move executes no edge or receipt.
Enabled wrong IN has its real latch/control/receipt successor. Wrong
bootstrap stores remain raw; unique well-typed noncanonical decoded rows
execute their real effects, and ill-typed/ambiguous rows have no enabled
main successor. All original invalid preparation/source packets, pending
IN-before-WR, EX blanks, partial banks/descriptors/allocations/copies,
version transitions, loan cuts, cursor/meter heads and halt remain physical
states. No positive conversion theorem is assigned to arbitrary raw input.

